"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkdocs=globalThis.webpackChunkdocs||[]).push([[5418],{930(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"ydesign-sdk-v1","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/blog/ydesign-sdk-v1","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2024-03-24-ydesign-sdk-v1.md","title":"Ydesign SDK v1.0 Officially Released \ud83c\udf89","description":"Hi everyone, this is BingheMori. After nearly two years of development and polishing, I\'m thrilled to announce: Ydesign SDK v1.0 is officially released! \ud83c\udf89","date":"2024-03-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Ydesign","permalink":"/en/blog/tags/ydesign","description":"News, releases and roadmap for the Ydesign product itself."},{"inline":false,"label":"Release notes","permalink":"/en/blog/tags/release","description":"Release notes for the SDK, component library, and core package."},{"inline":false,"label":"Canvas engine","permalink":"/en/blog/tags/canvas","description":"Posts about @ydesign/core, fabric.js, and canvas rendering."},{"inline":false,"label":"React","permalink":"/en/blog/tags/react","description":"Posts about the @ydesign/react-editor UI layer, MobX, and mobx-state-tree."},{"inline":false,"label":"Architecture","permalink":"/en/blog/tags/architecture","description":"Handler-based layering, monorepo, package boundaries, and engineering practices."},{"inline":false,"label":"Design editor","permalink":"/en/blog/tags/design-editor","description":"Industry observations, product thinking, and implementation lessons for online design tools."}],"readingTime":5.68,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"BingheMori","title":"Author of Ydesign Editor","url":"https://github.com/liaojunhao","page":{"permalink":"/en/blog/authors/binghe"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/liaojunhao"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/liaojunhao.png","key":"binghe"},{"name":"Klchan-me","title":"Former Senior Engineer at Tencent","url":"https://github.com/Klchan-me","page":{"permalink":"/en/blog/authors/klchan"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/Klchan-me"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/Klchan-me.png","key":"klchan"}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Ydesign SDK v1.0 Officially Released \ud83c\udf89","slug":"ydesign-sdk-v1","authors":["binghe","klchan"],"tags":["ydesign","release","canvas","react","architecture","design-editor"]},"unlisted":false},"content":"Hi everyone, this is **BingheMori**. After nearly two years of development and polishing, **I\'m thrilled to announce: Ydesign SDK v1.0 is officially released!** \ud83c\udf89\\n\\nStarting from this version, we\'ll strictly follow Semantic Versioning (SemVer), so your upgrades become more stable and predictable.\\n\\n_Of course, don\'t put blind faith in modern frontend \u2014 assume every upgrade might still surprise you in new ways._ \ud83d\ude05\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What is Ydesign SDK?\\n\\n**Ydesign SDK** is a collection of **JavaScript modules, component libraries, and React components** for building \\"Canva-style online design editors\\". It\'s already being used in production by multiple companies and indie developers.\\n\\nTypical scenarios where Ydesign SDK fits:\\n\\n1. **Design & social media apps** \u2014 posters, covers, business cards, video thumbnails\\n2. **Print-on-demand & product customization** \u2014 self-service designs for T-shirts, mugs, tote bags, phone cases\\n3. **E-commerce & online storefronts** \u2014 let buyers customize product artwork, generate marketing assets\\n\\n## The Story Behind It\\n\\nI\'ve been following the online design / canvas editor space for a long time. Over the past few years I worked on several products in positions similar to [canva.com](https://www.canva.com/) \u2014 some were direct competitors, some were just \\"self-service design\\" modules inside a bigger product, but the underlying implementation was strikingly similar.\\n\\nAfter a while it hit me: **the experience I\'ve accumulated over these years is already enough to build one myself.** Instead of rebuilding this kind of editor from scratch for someone else every time, I might as well crystallize the capability and build one for **myself**.\\n\\nBut the hard part isn\'t shipping the app. If the goal were a consumer-facing \\"Canva clone\\", most of the energy would go into marketing, user acquisition, and differentiation \u2014 a lane that\'s already crowded with competitors. I almost abandoned the idea.\\n\\nAfter a lot of thinking, a better angle emerged: **instead of a C-end Canva competitor, build a developer-facing B2B product.** Abstract \\"building a canvas editor\\" into a JS SDK so other developers can plug a fully-featured canvas editor into their own business. This direction matches my strengths \u2014 I\'ve spent years writing frameworks and tools, and I have a pretty good sense of what kind of APIs and docs actually feel good to use (I hope).\\n\\nYdesign SDK was born out of that.\\n\\nHuge thanks to my partner along the way, **[Klchan-me](https://github.com/Klchan-me)** (former senior engineer at Tencent, co-founder of LingxiGuangnian) \u2014 from architecture, performance, to engineering standards, walking this road together.\\n\\n## Quick Start\\n\\nYdesign SDK targets developers, so make sure you (or someone on your team) has an engineer who understands why `0.1 + 0.2 !== 0.3` \ud83d\ude09.\\n\\nIf reading docs while coding feels tedious, you can also clone the [`apps/demo`](https://github.com/liaojunhao/react-fabric-editor) example project in the repo and run it out of the box.\\n\\n### Install\\n\\n```bash\\nnpm install @ydesign/react-editor\\n```\\n\\n### Bootstrapping an editor in React\\n\\nThe snippet below isn\'t the most minimal, but it\'s a **copy-paste-ready starting template** you can gradually adapt to your business:\\n\\n```tsx\\nimport React from \'react\';\\nimport ReactDOM from \'react-dom/client\';\\nimport { DesignEditorContainer, SidePanelWrap, WorkspaceWrap, SidePanel, createStore } from \'@ydesign/react-editor\';\\nimport Toolbar from \'@ydesign/react-editor/toolbar\';\\nimport Workspace from \'@ydesign/react-editor/canvas/workspace\';\\nimport ZoomButtons from \'@ydesign/react-editor/toolbar/zoom-buttons\';\\n\\n// Don\'t forget the editor styles\\nimport \'@ydesign/react-editor/style.css\';\\n\\nconst store = createStore({\\n  // Get one from the console: https://ydesign.com/cabinet/\\n  key: \'LICENSE_KEY\',\\n  // A paid license allows you to hide the backlink,\\n  // but keeping it visible is a great way to support the Ydesign project.\\n  showCredit: true,\\n});\\nstore.addPage();\\n\\nexport const App = ({ store }) => {\\n  return (\\n    <DesignEditorContainer style={{ width: \'100vw\', height: \'100vh\' }}>\\n      <SidePanelWrap>\\n        <SidePanel store={store} />\\n      </SidePanelWrap>\\n      <WorkspaceWrap>\\n        <Toolbar store={store} />\\n        <Workspace store={store} />\\n        <ZoomButtons store={store} />\\n      </WorkspaceWrap>\\n    </DesignEditorContainer>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\n// Make sure there\'s a container with id `root` and an explicit size on the page\\nconst root = ReactDOM.createRoot(document.getElementById(\'root\'));\\nroot.render(<App store={store} />);\\n```\\n\\nIf you don\'t even want to write those few lines, there\'s an even more minimal entry point:\\n\\n```tsx\\nimport { createDesignEditorApp } from \'@ydesign/react-editor\';\\nimport \'@ydesign/react-editor/style.css\';\\n\\nconst { store } = createDesignEditorApp({\\n  container: document.getElementById(\'root\'),\\n  key: \'LICENSE_KEY\',\\n});\\n```\\n\\n## Commercialization\\n\\nAs of today, Ydesign is still an **independent project maintained by a small team**.\\n\\nFrom the very first line of code, it took roughly three months to reach a \\"proof of concept\\"; another six months or so to round out the core capabilities, ship the first public release, and put the website live; and a lot of time after that iterating on real customer feedback. Through this process we\'ve developed an increasingly clear answer to \\"what kind of design SDK developers actually want\\".\\n\\nBeyond polishing the SDK itself, Ydesign keeps the commercial layer intentionally lean: **Community** open-sources the universal canvas; differentiated capabilities ship as **plugin source licenses**; AI and cloud render are **usage-based**; customization and private deployment cover enterprise rollout. We do not center the business on \\"cheap whole-repo source dumps.\\" **Making the SDK great and improving the developer experience** remains the top priority.\\n\\nIf your team is evaluating a similar solution, feel free to reach out \u2014 we\'d love to chat.\\n\\n## What\'s Next?\\n\\nWe believe demand in this space will keep growing, and we\'ve already seen new competitors entering. There\'s still plenty on our backlog worth doing, mainly in these directions:\\n\\n1. **Official release of `@ydesign/vue-editor`** \u2014 so the Vue 3 ecosystem gets full parity with the React version, with JSON fully interoperable between the two.\\n2. **More export formats** \u2014 print-ready PDF, HTML, high-resolution SVG, etc.\\n3. **Extending the [cloud rendering API](/docs/export-import/cloud-render-api)** \u2014 solving performance and consistency for large files, high-resolution output, and batch rendering.\\n4. **Continuously expanding AI capabilities** \u2014 smart background removal, one-click layout, AI image generation, one-click variations, and more.\\n5. **Collaborative editing** \u2014 CRDT-based real-time multi-user collaboration.\\n6. **PPTX presentation editing** \u2014 import `.pptx` files, edit page by page, export back to `.pptx`, turning Ydesign into \\"PowerPoint in the browser\\". Built on top of the multi-page scene (`SceneHandler`) capability.\\n\\nFor the full roadmap, see the [Roadmap page](https://yitu.featurebase.app/roadmap). You\'re also welcome to vote and discuss on GitHub Discussions.\\n\\nAlongside the SDK, we also maintain an internal showcase app called **Ydesign Studio** \u2014 to demonstrate what the SDK is capable of, and as our own dogfooding playground. It was originally positioned as a \\"forever free, no-login, no-paywall\\" tool. But as we plan to integrate some expensive capabilities (e.g. image background removal), Ydesign Studio may also introduce a limited paywall \u2014 we\'re still thinking through how to keep the \\"minimum friction\\" experience while making it sustainable.\\n\\n> **If Ydesign has been helpful to you, giving us a Star on the [GitHub repo](https://github.com/liaojunhao/react-fabric-editor) is the best kind of support.**\\n\\n\x3c!-- Got feedback? Let us know on GitHub Issues: https://github.com/liaojunhao/react-fabric-editor/issues --\x3e"}]}}')}}]);